# RISC0 Error Conversion Standards

## Context
- Applied when working with RISC0 Result and Option types
- Focuses on proper error conversion between different error types
- Ensures consistent error handling patterns
- Critical for RISC0 v1.2.1 API usage

## Requirements

### Result and Option Handling
- Use pattern matching for Option to Result conversion:
  ```rust
  // GOOD: Use pattern matching
  match value.composite() {
      Some(cr) => Ok(cr),
      None => Err(anyhow::anyhow!("Expected composite receipt"))
  }
  
  // BAD: Don't use ok_or/ok_or_else with anyhow::Result
  value.composite().ok_or(anyhow::anyhow!("..."))  // Won't compile
  ```

### Error Type Conversion
- Convert RISC0 errors to anyhow errors using proper patterns:
  ```rust
  // GOOD: Use ? with From implementations
  prover.prove_session(&ctx, &session)?;
  
  // GOOD: Use explicit conversion with into()
  Err(Risc0RunnerError::ProofGenerationError.into())
  ```

### Error Context
- Add context to errors when converting:
  ```rust
  // GOOD: Add context during conversion
  match receipt.inner.composite() {
      Some(cr) => Ok(cr),
      None => {
          error!("Expected composite receipt in prove_session");
          Err(anyhow::anyhow!("Invalid receipt type"))
      }
  }
  ```

## Examples

<example>
// Good: Complete error handling with pattern matching
fn process_receipt(receipt: &Receipt) -> Result<Vec<u8>> {
    match receipt.inner.composite() {
        Some(cr) => {
            let sr = prover.composite_to_succinct(cr)?;
            Ok(sr.get_seal_bytes().to_vec())
        }
        None => {
            error!("Expected composite receipt");
            Err(anyhow::anyhow!("Invalid receipt type"))
        }
    }
}
</example>

<example>
// Good: Error conversion with context
fn verify_receipt(receipt: Receipt) -> Result<()> {
    match receipt.claim() {
        Ok(claim) => {
            match claim {
                MaybePruned::Value(rc) => Ok(()),
                MaybePruned::Pruned(_) => {
                    error!("Unexpected pruned claim");
                    Err(anyhow::anyhow!("Cannot verify pruned claim"))
                }
            }
        }
        Err(e) => {
            error!("Failed to get claim: {}", e);
            Err(e.into())
        }
    }
}
</example>

<example type="invalid">
// Bad: Using ok_or with anyhow::Result
fn process_unsafe(receipt: &Receipt) -> Result<Vec<u8>> {
    receipt.inner.composite()
        .ok_or(anyhow::anyhow!("Expected composite"))? // Won't compile
        .get_seal_bytes()
        .to_vec()
}
</example>

<example type="invalid">
// Bad: Missing error context
fn verify_unsafe(receipt: Receipt) -> Result<()> {
    receipt.claim()?.as_value()?; // Missing context
    Ok(())
}
</example>

## Critical Notes
<critical>
- NEVER use ok_or/ok_or_else with anyhow::Result
- ALWAYS use pattern matching for Option to Result conversion
- Add appropriate error context during conversion
- Use proper error type conversion methods
- Include logging for error cases
- Maintain consistent error handling patterns
</critical> 